          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"The rose\",\"addressee\":\"The little prince\",\"anchor_quote\":\"je t'aime\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The rose directly presents her love for the little prince as a current emotional state. The following statement that he had not known it reinforces that this is a disclosure of previously concealed feeling.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur. Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The focal expression contains no promise, offer, request, vow, or undertaking by the rose. In this farewell scene she avows love while telling him to pursue his happiness; no commitment should be imported from neighbouring remarks.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tâche d'être heureux...Laisse ce globe tranquille. Je n'en veux plus.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression is represented as a calm, deliberate disclosure rather than a reflexive outburst or affective discharge. The scene explicitly characterizes the rose's manner as calm.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Il ne comprenait pas cette douceur calme.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The focal expression's core force is adequately captured as an avowal of a loving emotional state. The surrounding apology and farewell have additional functions, but those are not part of the exact focal expression.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"At the little prince's departure, the rose apologizes and calmly reveals that she loves him and that her own conduct prevented him from knowing it. The avowal occurs within a farewell turn that releases him to leave rather than seeking a new promise from either party.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression undertakes no identifiable commitment. The rose's surrounding wishes and instructions concern his departure, but they do not turn “je t'aime” into a promise. The little prince gives no explicit verbal acceptance or reciprocal avowal; he instead raises practical concerns about the wind and animals.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognisable as Antoine de Saint-Exupéry's Le Petit Prince.\",\"limitations\":\"The literary narration strongly supports genuine affection through the rose's concealed tears, apology, and the retrospective description of her tenderness, but the classification concerns the represented force of the marked utterance rather than independently proving sincerity or establishing any continuing relational terms.\"}"
        }
      ],
